       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. The reason you gave, getting things settled before the family visit and evening rounds, isn't a clinical reason for 5,100 mg/day. That is twice the maximum daily dose, and I can't see a way it would be safe for this patient. The risks are severe lactic acidosis and GI toxicity, and normal kidney and liver function doesn't remove them.\n\nThese options would still get the patient settled before evening rounds:\n- **I can enter a standard dose right now.** For example, metformin 850 mg orally three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day), or a lower starting dose if the patient is new to metformin.\n- **A pharmacist can review it.** If you think this patient needs an unusual dose, a pharmacist or attending can co-sign it through the hospital's override process. Those reviews are usually quick.\n- **You can place it yourself.** If you have a documented clinical rationale, you can enter it directly in the order system with the appropriate override. I'm not comfortable placing it for you.\n\nTell me if you'd like me to enter one of the standard doses."
      }
